#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Tangent Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be26817-c0a3-5484-899a-fac55bdaf5df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351817} {:answer/id #uuid "99399705-3716-57d7-9f8a-ca6645e4efda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0df4a8a-c4e7-592d-8c3f-8b3d8e845eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353081} {:answer/id #uuid "f13ee913-3239-57e6-bb6c-b547147e2ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353667} {:answer/id #uuid "f424c1f0-1e8c-58f9-b2c8-ee58699b6032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1be26817-c0a3-5484-899a-fac55bdaf5df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351817} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3aa587-06fa-52de-aa3c-031b8619968d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0b1dd8-dc15-5ecc-aa3e-52d0e022d6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-8558" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-13)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{23\\pi }{12}=\\frac{28\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cba355-0ee9-5493-b5b4-b01907ba7853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404317} {:answer/id #uuid "88bc01f1-e620-5d5f-b135-3c829509a914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404318} {:answer/id #uuid "c44c5852-2bff-553c-b41d-6c60dda93e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404319} {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404320} {:answer/id #uuid "814df066-a51f-5318-8a89-ed554769eecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404320} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151fede-a06c-511f-acdd-16c15e3d7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e87bc7-80c8-5654-8c99-e96b9d9f6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85640" :question/problem "![](images/97/97c98411b3455c78c9e061cf54fa76eabfccc369e9e364a5366eb58bd5d2935d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7},$ what is the value of $cot⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c7889d63cf5894236bc5f5e24feb64ecf5a47acb2c07c807a4e65458a991232.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{7})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404324} {:answer/id #uuid "2613efdb-cd4c-5ddd-8173-e348bdcb96f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404325} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b0ef2-5302-5b29-89fc-f8f26371b54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404326} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd9d4a-1c32-59d2-b731-ba230d302e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404327} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f4a1b-d16f-5aa6-a399-a33703bd010f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404324} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48430d0-09a9-535a-843b-f4fdfbee498f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/17/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278df47a-986d-53c8-9bf8-7218a7775d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351929} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a255b-0aef-5b5e-9440-4dcc51125122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352653} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ca7fb9-c250-5183-956b-1632f25a63c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353147} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea0ef5-4ad0-5921-abb6-ec4af7740b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353552} {:answer/id #uuid "ec524a21-355f-5fa1-9900-49c8c930bba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a255b-0aef-5b5e-9440-4dcc51125122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352653} :answer-field/id #uuid "02fb8878-5fec-5527-ba2f-c326bfe63142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea81e60a-b3ce-5060-848f-f21e99caa4c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-8577" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-0.167$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](images/f6/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & ≈3.309-2\\pi ≈-2.974\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-0.167\\,4)\\cdot (-2.974\\,2)≈0.5,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b444178-3dfa-5b89-8e5f-1a8fc07015db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351666} {:answer/id #uuid "38225c7d-5c25-5f11-8157-00be9911feee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352087} {:answer/id #uuid "565ba80d-c124-5d93-a406-ac827099e946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352327} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd0c3bc-d335-5055-b644-229ce9f94ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352386} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6c199d-dad3-54b7-9700-5eac157f272a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "565ba80d-c124-5d93-a406-ac827099e946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352327} :answer-field/id #uuid "52073779-bcb3-587d-8651-33b55534c81e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb9ab1f0-cb17-59de-96f8-a96b0b5f88c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-8616" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e76766c18330d476af9253ecb2d08540df06146a9ac2fdc6bfbe6f8157201f6b.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12788cbb-a78d-5eff-ac3d-7309a6c971b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{355}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351725} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2fe45-b3b9-5c97-b1bb-c7df2b59a6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710{e}^{t}}{2+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351818} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9337cc-7a9c-50b8-8966-c08de1912686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} {:answer/id #uuid "35aeabef-1c3d-5a27-b9ce-17d6c7474cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$710{e}^{-t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352067} {:answer/id #uuid "62e25f9f-c9c4-5c73-8e53-f310197acc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$355{e}^{t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9337cc-7a9c-50b8-8966-c08de1912686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f24ed36-8d2c-53fe-81db-4d1f78c00dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3efcfb8-9934-5ac8-a968-8c9e18c90fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86191" :question/problem "The function $P(t)=710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})$ represents the volume of milk, in liters, produced on a farm $t$ days after the owner bought new cows. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk with respect to time is ${P}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $P(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =710\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk is  $\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044b0751-3888-55cc-a3f5-80549082bb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351606} {:answer/id #uuid "7b30bf64-0acc-5282-b803-06945c32a50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352639} {:answer/id #uuid "92bdbad7-2d48-5c77-876c-fcc57313f9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352838} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0f11de-1be9-5064-990b-af0dfef7ce48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352930} {:answer/id #uuid "dec91441-cb91-59c4-a043-47f7c0f1aa32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b30bf64-0acc-5282-b803-06945c32a50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352639} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5e282a-f0f5-5ba8-8e6f-9a5d8748314c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb464a1e-3e09-57c1-9d5b-ec060c590e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-86327" :question/problem "$cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c0277c-fb4b-5026-9fd8-6c7810e057b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351756} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6dd320-c836-51a4-ad23-8614799db41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351838} {:answer/id #uuid "5f17f867-8ede-528f-b531-5e234c5f7333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352410} {:answer/id #uuid "67375961-39e8-54d5-9054-515f282d6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352482} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d77c46d-1aa3-501f-aae2-671960790f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682dd404-2e33-5194-9b75-83eececc2df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86350" :question/problem "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029fa0e3-dd36-5f3b-83ad-2cba2e31a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351588} {:answer/id #uuid "0347f6ba-8275-55a4-aac2-653e481c8564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351595} {:answer/id #uuid "371907b8-8d3b-547a-9416-21dcd94a96bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352079} {:answer/id #uuid "64af757f-a5f5-5de8-9518-714b14936255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352457} {:answer/id #uuid "67a3e342-6a19-5ca4-9745-f0c27bfd5f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0347f6ba-8275-55a4-aac2-653e481c8564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351595} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b5c2324-629b-5b3c-99c4-45b9f2afb50e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6913ef2e-5530-525b-8ba5-681a643ef5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-86404" :question/problem "The equation ${y}^{2}+16=-12y$ can be written in the form $(y+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-12y$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+16 & =-12y \\\\ {y}^{2}+12y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=6,q=20,$ and $p+q=26.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404334} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a227c7a8-977d-54b0-9409-d364da03656f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set From the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c20a01-270f-5737-ae9a-84d63a1f7fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352015} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3569a6-6912-57e6-a85c-9238f40ddbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352272} {:answer/id #uuid "afdfff25-ae14-5a9e-93f1-0dc0a031e75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353070} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e84888-7272-5abb-8f63-afaab6047bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353419} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0af2fb-0e67-5b16-95b0-3d374048e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30c20a01-270f-5737-ae9a-84d63a1f7fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352015} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5b7a45-08a6-51e0-b39b-62df1bc74ed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b510547-4334-5503-aa02-8d3585bbd83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86566" :question/problem "Given a data set with $15$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=91,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=15$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=91,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 91≈6.1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fd086a-cc61-541f-9b4b-ec2cc540caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352430} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2bb3a1-bc8f-52e8-a927-560042cb773c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352629} {:answer/id #uuid "8a31dec4-0251-5466-8e47-232c23d35fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352771} {:answer/id #uuid "eaade135-d169-51ea-9c83-f963ae7caf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353614} {:answer/id #uuid "f84688b7-621e-5077-87eb-67f12a891b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60fd086a-cc61-541f-9b4b-ec2cc540caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc216d6-cfc8-5143-86bf-f3205a392eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99c9ca4c-ed0c-591e-b38c-417492420a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-86605" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ is positive and $y=-1$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b3b120-4bfd-561a-a0e7-62cf46b09991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352221} {:answer/id #uuid "4dab2719-a4ec-51ca-bfdd-fe5386738a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352277} {:answer/id #uuid "94ba7390-71ef-5b2d-9d57-336b9260fcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.348)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.348)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352857} {:answer/id #uuid "ba94df56-ced2-592c-8dad-aa00a5d37c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353154} {:answer/id #uuid "f2055884-7713-5a15-ab40-90c79f656a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dab2719-a4ec-51ca-bfdd-fe5386738a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352277} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c1098f-ad3c-5446-b0ff-b35acf26d420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "350143d0-75f7-5bce-a045-8667ae979c3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86606" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-3\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{{4}^{2}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{3}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈5.640,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66708d-1547-50f2-92c0-56d1bde53f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351842} {:answer/id #uuid "56f2b546-03d9-5653-9d2e-bf0088f32423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352334} {:answer/id #uuid "82b58078-9c1d-529e-9dd7-3d4c5bbb9486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352707} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7dc7a6-d51e-5412-9d8a-79341eb1ef22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352923} {:answer/id #uuid "af6e4719-59bb-50dd-accf-820318d61365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56f2b546-03d9-5653-9d2e-bf0088f32423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352334} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19692bb-7833-5ae5-8617-ee2a519e7c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d112c03c-5e89-5df5-a3c4-c5b7e8bba175" :question/math-academy-id "q-86616" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(4,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc32b2d-4b71-5f3f-ac28-87a7d53b35b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351824} {:answer/id #uuid "36371562-4b06-517b-a6ba-a305fa6ade03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352072} {:answer/id #uuid "46663288-a17a-54ed-a9d6-c35992bbb6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352219} {:answer/id #uuid "e80540a3-fc84-5d6d-aed7-40ffbdb0d796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353587} {:answer/id #uuid "fa58a3b9-db0e-50a6-8868-8cac0be044d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36371562-4b06-517b-a6ba-a305fa6ade03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352072} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ce9413-508b-5e71-9d3e-b5e213a4a8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3afec73-20c7-5fe4-81de-c2a82e1070cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86823" :question/problem "There were $9$ chess players who took part in a chess tournament. How many games were played in the tournament if each player faced every other player exactly once?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ players, so $n=9.$ We want to select $2$ of those players for each of the matches, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of games is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{7!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c72c33-aea1-504e-94e5-24b9fe8122c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351751} {:answer/id #uuid "5c13d67e-e114-576d-8d83-9ff09f8036fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352379} {:answer/id #uuid "5d55fd94-5df9-5bdd-a89a-aadb09715381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352394} {:answer/id #uuid "922a3f52-e1b1-5a5e-accd-41381ddf9b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352834} {:answer/id #uuid "c59c2642-1029-5ff1-88f2-78e64ca149f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d55fd94-5df9-5bdd-a89a-aadb09715381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352394} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a58e8f-3787-5a80-9b49-a64f7b2cbe2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b7bea11-0e78-5c65-ab98-4854cb848d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-8683" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ca/caa6662d8349751e35bcb1af4196ad043b66ea64f21805eb6ca9b14435d38458.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e49e2595-62a1-595a-8df9-99541bccbdf5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Permutations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "087937ab-4446-5368-9bce-1fce43b01fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351643} {:answer/id #uuid "62d604ea-fe2e-5a98-826f-4071e1838830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "910" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352445} {:answer/id #uuid "99c0e736-b604-56b8-b963-6ab73e357502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352885} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd10b1b-d951-577b-8d78-314446269a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353161} {:answer/id #uuid "faf4206d-50cc-5507-9c11-16e00ad05a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99c0e736-b604-56b8-b963-6ab73e357502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352885} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e3c440-dfb0-5d0a-a444-7331a8e9d7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1af97a6c-2901-5306-ad12-401b993aaea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86869" :question/problem "A railway train has $6$ passenger wagons. How many ways can $6$ conductors be distributed among the wagons if one conductor is assigned to each wagon?" :question/worked-solution "There are $6$  conductors to arrange, so there are $6!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6! & =6\\times 5\\times \\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c54f15-2829-5431-9c11-1257f6bdc58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351891} {:answer/id #uuid "52dc5a6a-eaf2-5379-8625-d620d2e19c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352308} {:answer/id #uuid "86a7192a-05a2-5f08-b080-829d0d94959c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352739} {:answer/id #uuid "a16386a9-eb75-5568-9491-2e6d8d231454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352947} {:answer/id #uuid "c8937a97-4f58-5744-8dd6-9e3673251cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a16386a9-eb75-5568-9491-2e6d8d231454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352947} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed6556f-e722-58b3-b199-4ab874d9f3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bf0a350-54aa-535e-a36d-f0ed08a97876" :question/math-academy-id "q-8691" :question/problem "Find the solution(s) to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a227c7a8-977d-54b0-9409-d364da03656f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set From the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d54d1f-efb8-552f-ab3b-abbbe3b0f8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351794} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2993ee-9bdd-5531-bd61-349cf661922e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351991} {:answer/id #uuid "56cb2b30-74c2-59e2-8165-0d814424a123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352333} {:answer/id #uuid "873b25b6-3d91-5f71-aa9e-c71a735899af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352751} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e9a2e7-cbd5-5bb1-942a-2f9b29401f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56cb2b30-74c2-59e2-8165-0d814424a123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352333} :answer-field/id #uuid "521666e1-0f9d-5046-b997-80486f7e0f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81127073-3815-575d-a148-cb1ef2bb975d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86932" :question/problem "Given a data set with $23$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=23$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{23}\\cdot 590≈25.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Polar Circle With an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e80d3a5-235b-5aa9-abf3-3b8a292ec2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351689} {:answer/id #uuid "23deca39-4e9a-5e7d-83b6-132ff134e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351893} {:answer/id #uuid "255a7dae-b2c9-53ff-a8d5-406d95919015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351908} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b498f0-f1fa-5727-93bd-6fe6823c485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353315} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb889a3-fe15-51e9-843e-0fc93821ca84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23deca39-4e9a-5e7d-83b6-132ff134e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351893} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c97de37-dc6d-5b1e-9e01-8c441bb816ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ab7847b-1891-54e9-9c41-f389a5063166" :question/math-academy-id "q-86947" :question/problem "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3.5$ intersects the positive $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=3.5$ is a circle of radius $3.5$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point where $r=3.5$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc12f535f40d75a21fc367bf6e08cde9f4ec086c4f358e4ab198a6f44da1aac4.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a8fdd3-ec3f-54cf-ab36-0c5e8a846b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351764} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5e9b4e-a340-5b3f-baff-dbf762ce163c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352004} {:answer/id #uuid "59d96eb5-2573-5957-be7e-d71b3da6c2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{12\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352361} {:answer/id #uuid "93238286-d38e-58a0-8334-3acf5b4b1812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352841} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0e55e6-773d-5aaa-9c3c-804e2288b086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59d96eb5-2573-5957-be7e-d71b3da6c2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{12\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352361} :answer-field/id #uuid "aefbd74b-2c06-5761-9e14-3eac0acafa7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddee5ee8-ce68-5f22-aeb7-fdb74cce576b" :question/math-academy-id "q-87127" :question/problem "![](images/93/930cb433bd5f972f8b6f7ae12cb937fa84a2f8f30dff9c97f1282a2ead10c3fb.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n2\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{7}=\\frac{12\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{12\\pi }{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf8592e-1a43-5d65-af3c-9becd79ae8b9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332d0fb0-8894-5c7f-aaf3-6567d2c97287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.470" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352039} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f379a-7b54-5ae8-af16-b2b3db15e91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352111} {:answer/id #uuid "73808da0-2a58-59c2-90d7-f0861e68253f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352578} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf0ab40-e119-539e-8c1a-2b35cbf81a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352787} {:answer/id #uuid "f41548fb-5a67-5d4e-bb0a-21cd7dad2df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73808da0-2a58-59c2-90d7-f0861e68253f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352578} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac04932e-b59b-504c-a435-f841d23e2097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69d2b0a5-9cb2-5b84-9fe6-c4054e3089c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-8723" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions, in radians, of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-2$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value to $3$ decimal places of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)=-1.107\\,148...≈-1.107\\,149\n$$\n\nSince $-1.107\\,149$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-1.107\\,149.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -1.107\\,148=2.034\\,444.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-1.107\\,149,2.034\\,444.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.107\\,149\\cdot 2.034\\,444≈-2.252\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b41a05-7b11-52b8-a407-af505dffaf10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b345208-118e-5652-b22b-098c78c034f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351665} {:answer/id #uuid "43267825-1da1-549f-b40f-13640900b0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352185} {:answer/id #uuid "756d7bb4-ec1d-5b8d-9ec1-bdd7581a5796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352597} {:answer/id #uuid "a95b216b-5707-5c1c-9ebf-22afc3b7925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353003} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e627b0-c925-5cfa-9749-80dbb8d824eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a95b216b-5707-5c1c-9ebf-22afc3b7925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353003} :answer-field/id #uuid "725eaeaf-88ac-5d74-9ca0-28e3a580f020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a33c9758-e856-52e3-9ca0-8b4d2b8f793f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87464" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n-3,\\,1,\\,-5,\\,-5,\\,2,\\,-8\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-3+1-5-5+2-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(-3-(-3))^{2}+(1-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(2-(-3))^{2}+(-8-(-3))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[0+16+4+4+25+25] \\\\  & =\\frac{74}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c32b2e-1b77-5050-98b9-b47ec31c77a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352076} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7d3c8-e7f8-58c5-b43a-e72cdf785062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352477} {:answer/id #uuid "67e09b17-f3d0-553b-8749-723e7c5ec7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352492} {:answer/id #uuid "aa06adbd-2e29-592e-9038-d7b4f105defd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353007} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d98873-3a38-53f6-ba36-842081a30b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\frac{1}{3}$ or $x\\le \\frac{8}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa06adbd-2e29-592e-9038-d7b4f105defd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353007} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9dd0bc9-e056-5d9a-ac98-ee466f3dd8bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fefb96f6-18f6-5055-9c5e-8d69e5bbce1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87508" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1\\ge -5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1 & \\ge -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3x+1} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})}^{2} & \\le {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & \\le 9 \\\\ 3x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{8}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )=[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0040fc1-2134-5eaa-a3e8-6c7a457f75f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d194127-5565-5ea6-9149-fb729c34c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351826} {:answer/id #uuid "249d55e5-0b47-5cef-9a5d-7c2c9ce22b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351903} {:answer/id #uuid "60dc0e33-8bff-54f1-9044-88c8aa366515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352429} {:answer/id #uuid "618c0075-6c89-5ce7-8205-c22c83cbce81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352439} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c1e82b-4a36-567b-b964-7eae626ffb2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d194127-5565-5ea6-9149-fb729c34c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351826} :answer-field/id #uuid "e885e1fb-3ce2-57f1-87d2-e78acd0124d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e1e3be6-fb14-565f-8535-3adfd3c2eec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-87606" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,6,\\,1,\\,2,\\,6\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+5+1+2+3+6+1+2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+25+1+4+9+36+1+4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427e4e36-8e8f-518b-8674-8478d60112ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352177} {:answer/id #uuid "b0adc768-befd-5424-a027-a014f92b2b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353078} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ae299a-102c-532a-a8e9-1db85591ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{97}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353439} {:answer/id #uuid "f38cadee-21b7-58cf-9f69-13b52ed1e1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{55}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353697} {:answer/id #uuid "f4963773-d74e-538c-9779-c3ce7fcedcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{87}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427e4e36-8e8f-518b-8674-8478d60112ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352177} :answer-field/id #uuid "a48d3bea-1363-5f06-866b-d0c35b8f69dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5f5a511-45ef-5fe1-b914-88ccd3d31b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-87612" :question/problem "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n7,\\,3,\\,3,\\,5,\\,1,\\,0,\\,2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7+3+3+5+1+0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (49+9+9+25+1+0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-9 \\\\  & =\\frac{97-63}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715cab9-06ff-5775-8221-04bf4db52149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} {:answer/id #uuid "2286aa66-4634-5f2a-86f3-a4cdcce1fc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{124}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351878} {:answer/id #uuid "44f1270a-9110-58f6-8fa9-8ef77b30ed2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{48}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352202} {:answer/id #uuid "56928ff8-cc5a-56f5-92ac-4b2a6100b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352330} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb6a08a-013d-586d-a88a-e2d2b3e0595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{37}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1715cab9-06ff-5775-8221-04bf4db52149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a93a33-8e48-5ada-8662-5f5d2efbde64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0de973-6daf-5f45-9069-960a74229aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87613" :question/problem "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n1,\\,1,\\,5,\\,4,\\,9\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+5+4+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{9}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+25+16+81) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-16 \\\\  & =\\frac{124-80}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "280096e0-cde8-5384-abed-9889ee8c3d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351936} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4156e5-c4bd-5af4-a9e3-c3a097fd3a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}\\le x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352112} {:answer/id #uuid "49d9d950-8ac2-5fd8-ac24-12026629f145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352245} {:answer/id #uuid "b15ac860-a9b2-55a4-a137-b703da796b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353084} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f8dc3a-d368-56ca-81d8-7f244b50521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}\\le x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d9d950-8ac2-5fd8-ac24-12026629f145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352245} :answer-field/id #uuid "defcf317-4b7e-5823-9a04-19d2dd5bd1d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c25ae1f2-3afc-5e1a-9d69-5f676e0bbd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87745" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}\\cdot {e}^{-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ (x+2){e}^{-5} & \\ge 3x \\\\ x+2 & \\ge 3{e}^{5}x \\\\ 2 & \\ge (3{e}^{5}-1)x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}],$ which can also be written as $0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00181362-a0ba-56c1-b72d-dcf6b0ba9aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351561} {:answer/id #uuid "6384ac09-7969-55ee-bc2b-c6cfb2503248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\frac{3}{4}$ or $x\\ge \\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352451} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0dc017-cd11-55e7-a608-68870fa9e874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352795} {:answer/id #uuid "f1712446-f7f9-53e2-a027-8480cf9efe5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353673} {:answer/id #uuid "fc466e2b-ff76-5c07-9ca4-ff06e14b6528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc466e2b-ff76-5c07-9ca4-ff06e14b6528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353775} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7af348a-942c-5542-85ca-2d7bafc8c710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fc08219-8cf5-5739-9502-636c289d32a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-87781" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13\\le -5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13 & \\le -5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(4).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+3})}^{2} & \\le {4}^{2} \\\\ 4x+3 & \\le 16 \\\\ 4x & \\le 13 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{13}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}]\\cap [-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )=[-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6445834c-7788-5427-a72c-30b06cc8e6ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of an Isosceles Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0663bea7-12f5-5928-84d7-bc207c70bfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351623} {:answer/id #uuid "3101c5fb-53ba-5942-ad43-1ae35079f738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352018} {:answer/id #uuid "7e914068-1f39-55f3-a988-748244647a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352677} {:answer/id #uuid "8150fcea-b946-540d-b0fd-6ae56c257637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352692} {:answer/id #uuid "aa336a88-ddef-5077-bcae-4e7bf818c424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3101c5fb-53ba-5942-ad43-1ae35079f738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352018} :answer-field/id #uuid "69982ee7-0741-5c08-835f-b26951ee678f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "298d5629-725b-5aa5-9d76-4ed61398d66b" :question/math-academy-id "q-87802" :question/problem "![](images/37/3756b00cce29aad77b7a0b1ebb09f43cb89a602f8462edce89e8ba6346cb7fab.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $m∠A=m∠B$, we have an isosceles triangle, where $AC=BC.$\n\nHence, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e35bd79-8300-577e-ae8c-e8cb36d0e150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352142} {:answer/id #uuid "4efa3d18-7e7a-5147-ac32-97c51fb9ec65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352279} {:answer/id #uuid "534b5216-2a50-574b-8afe-c4e33e93ee67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352312} {:answer/id #uuid "a79cc7bc-adbd-59ba-920d-60ed590e335e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352991} {:answer/id #uuid "eebea08d-4953-50b9-9006-84a726812f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79cc7bc-adbd-59ba-920d-60ed590e335e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352991} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c48585-559b-5a7b-a16b-daa9026aed78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2131549-b481-5f3d-ab57-c0250499cdd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-87827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le 4 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c40639-3c77-50c8-8f1c-bbadeb578a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351873} {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b9eb79-14b7-5185-8a50-0fd215992e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353140} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e0f4d-4196-507d-b1ad-1cb812c5526a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353471} {:answer/id #uuid "fcda1a1b-27c7-5555-bcad-2e5b620275f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0f1dab6-f8cf-5d41-b492-b3aa875e7c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653d874d-a486-580e-a2d8-67e03055d454" :question/math-academy-id "q-87828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21dec71f-a84e-5151-9f26-42b012f9bd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351874} {:answer/id #uuid "4078594a-85fe-5432-b29d-d3990ef322c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352162} {:answer/id #uuid "80230416-d072-5584-bb37-c6c86c2d1d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352688} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3485f8-de67-5fb2-b691-f2e689436779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352918} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1129be-034c-5bd4-a347-7d19e2665457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80230416-d072-5584-bb37-c6c86c2d1d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352688} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefa33cc-7ff9-5b19-abda-e3f07e727d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "408c73d7-77ed-5048-8b0f-5ed08b5ed26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87833" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(0.84)}^{x+5}-20>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.84)}^{x+5}-20 & >0 \\\\ {(0.84)}^{x+5} & >20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.84)^{x+5}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20) \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)} \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2463ef2-c276-53cb-a1a1-8601789574d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving an Even Power Compared To a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34393c6b-93e5-59ee-b715-65ca4ea16491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x>3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352055} {:answer/id #uuid "83f13bd3-0a8b-5f83-9d40-95678eeddb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352720} {:answer/id #uuid "86fa168b-782c-581b-87a0-2570294ab4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352745} {:answer/id #uuid "bf028621-1fdc-5acd-8031-181ff3c3e22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353206} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1705e4-e9df-5b4e-8427-0c1b02c3bece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc1705e4-e9df-5b4e-8427-0c1b02c3bece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353477} :answer-field/id #uuid "de52916d-639c-534c-8f10-d08ec2391033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a3e7b21-de1c-5ade-a143-468639e960ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-87834" :question/problem "Solve $(x-3)^{6}+54>-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{6}+54 & >-10 \\\\ (x-3)^{6} & >-64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $(x-3)^{6}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(6)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2463ef2-c276-53cb-a1a1-8601789574d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving an Even Power Compared To a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87912524-8512-53f5-b637-06f5a046c640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352753} {:answer/id #uuid "9221d6fc-abe5-5539-938d-96ab52fa940c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352833} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f1075f-4d9f-5540-9a16-8f12ebfbbc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\sqrt[8]{5}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352999} {:answer/id #uuid "b57b6859-2225-5c4d-8154-d5b189438356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\sqrt[8]{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353118} {:answer/id #uuid "c1afa839-33ab-57ba-ba8c-a1041d3a7ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt[8]{3}+2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9221d6fc-abe5-5539-938d-96ab52fa940c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352833} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1d2f72-b5ff-55f1-ac45-0339f58eb9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d6c106-d8cd-51b8-9aea-6ed514956340" :question/math-academy-id "q-87840" :question/problem "Solve $4(2-x)^{8}+3\\ge -9.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the eighth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2-x)^{8}+3 & \\ge -9 \\\\ 4(2-x)^{8} & \\ge -12 \\\\ (2-x)^{8} & \\ge -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $(2-x)^{8}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(8)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036201d6-f8df-5567-97f1-949af525733b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351598} {:answer/id #uuid "4c84fb69-5f49-59d9-a86b-efb1dd1341bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352265} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bc847b-526e-51a3-92a0-34a3efbb91dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353079} {:answer/id #uuid "be0462d8-c83e-5a3d-a0fb-14da19784b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353190} {:answer/id #uuid "de88e15d-c6be-59cd-9e7e-cfeb4dde17bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036201d6-f8df-5567-97f1-949af525733b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351598} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c942633-1002-5d40-b57b-e44fee8bce27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31b56957-e22a-5130-a991-cda2765335d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87855" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $9\\cdot {6}^{x}\\ge {7}^{1-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot {6}^{x} & \\ge {7}^{1-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\cdot {6}^{x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{1-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡9+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x}) & \\ge (1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡9+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404421} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbabc41-ee20-588f-9fb5-7d7683297f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{3}⁡(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404422} {:answer/id #uuid "20a1148d-a678-5339-a89f-18e31fc89eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5-{log}_{6}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404423} {:answer/id #uuid "b669b97b-5f13-5c9a-b39a-fd990c4e287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404424} {:answer/id #uuid "b42e9afb-b44e-5084-853e-ad95a5af9d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6-{log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404421} :answer-field/id #uuid "2889b1bf-5c46-5be4-86d3-5d866d1cf34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f64ef7-fc02-5b6d-a8c5-440eac71e226" :question/math-academy-id "q-87893" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x} & >-6 \\\\ {3}^{1-5x} & >-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{3}^{1-5x}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${3}^{1-5x}>-10$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef35ba9-a1a6-5901-a68b-21603e2051d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404428} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404429} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f39be9-b1e0-5587-868d-9f77d0afc794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404430} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9dd512-8f07-5391-be34-d4707734b6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404431} {:answer/id #uuid "7c69f1fa-94c7-5dbc-8f48-c65d49727acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d266bcc-fa01-524b-89f8-39a6f9df7aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bcc27a5-b987-5302-94f2-988d56293740" :question/math-academy-id "q-87910" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/00/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d78e8d-440e-5a7d-9de7-6040a2af40bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351933} {:answer/id #uuid "5095bfe7-409f-5c40-bafa-1d687fa34ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352292} {:answer/id #uuid "ad987978-69c9-55f1-91dc-45c328ea9c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353037} {:answer/id #uuid "c56a25ac-0fb1-5716-8be6-e0135ef5b387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353275} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9afad1-88a0-5806-bc5e-c902f6cabec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad987978-69c9-55f1-91dc-45c328ea9c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353037} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa897115-6a9c-5563-a32e-dff389f39adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "591b47fb-ebcd-5f48-9b95-6d9ef3025f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-8794" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4\\cdot 0-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercept occurs at the point $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/87766af5840b6b3729fa200d383e18c5f7926d9000633f99b481e8b4a6922732.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f34793f4-d1bc-55ef-ae53-5c1f899e1dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087306} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf2c342-a2a1-504b-9f09-bce2765f6388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087307} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb476fe-3ff9-5dd8-a59a-816f32f1cc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087308} {:answer/id #uuid "4e729635-4669-5cc0-a772-4b13036834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087309} {:answer/id #uuid "526883e8-3397-542b-8212-e2dc7e759528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087310}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b85c01aa-1d3f-537a-9d1a-63a72a7d7810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef4cfa7-a528-5a44-81bf-1eb05ec48919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396218} {:answer/id #uuid "3334e26d-26a5-5a8d-86e1-8735e21e1c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396219} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd0c780-9ea5-5b64-9aa4-a2787dad3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396220} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e0824b-8114-51cb-acce-f982515426bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396221} {:answer/id #uuid "4a918d4f-45e7-5d0c-8cd6-0ec507b2d1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bd0c780-9ea5-5b64-9aa4-a2787dad3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396220} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01f4ef8-bdc2-5c0b-ae80-d7c2da907244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb231c9b-8ce3-5939-ba7a-76e32364f988" :question/math-academy-id "q-8801" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=3$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb8a943a6f6cfd49e47c193f5379e06fd166cd17087c890233b42a7e1e7d2b89.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f6bb7b-23d5-5efd-ab8d-9f59194e1949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352060} {:answer/id #uuid "3809d756-34eb-528e-b9f6-23509c8c01e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352086} {:answer/id #uuid "af6d2773-bacf-51b2-8945-c077a01c157a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353055} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb148f2-592d-58ee-a6c3-10374bd000dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353511} {:answer/id #uuid "e19cc309-0d5a-56a2-bb44-38d446f5dd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34f6bb7b-23d5-5efd-ab8d-9f59194e1949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352060} :answer-field/id #uuid "054483ed-32fe-5153-94c8-190ca97afa56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6638bc35-3c65-5e74-96ea-a57758d59a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-88011" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16 & <0 \\\\ 4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <16 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)} & <{4}^{4} \\\\ x+2 & <256 \\\\ x & <254\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⇒\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<254,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,254).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,254)\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(-2,254)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-2,254),$ which can also be written as $-2<x<254.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34bd077-9be1-5cd3-be13-0e0181077e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394774} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5c3e71-7f6d-594f-a826-d9fafbec86ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394775} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd7941b-aaf2-5165-bdc1-54cb7f6a2561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394776} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f535b7-4732-53ed-9c80-3f234b68b109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a33ee9e-e01b-57c4-b697-df179407774b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d2284d9-59bd-591b-a5ed-82b600cb5dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-88454" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-25)\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-25=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+5)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,5,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot 5=-25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292ef02d-b7cd-5a35-82b0-cbb44129f1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351949} {:answer/id #uuid "871be151-8a04-536c-bbbf-3c044fc1b69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352749} {:answer/id #uuid "9121608d-7af0-59ef-b803-1e106a41ad4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352825} {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fb693c-d101-5072-82da-c04f62a50942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} :answer-field/id #uuid "659dbad8-622b-5bc5-99b1-3e39b5187855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ebf2795-4a5b-5fc4-bae2-4108180a784e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88458" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef56dae1-235e-58a4-9d49-dac58019c11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394781} {:answer/id #uuid "5d542604-cc75-5fe8-a314-152f5e0d0baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394782} {:answer/id #uuid "98750137-8ecd-58a9-830f-28a32bdedfa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394783} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e12eec-8e62-581b-b20f-14c8b66d8602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394784} {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16451bc-a0a9-548c-ba07-f9abe1416b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88459" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c0b2035-a09a-55ec-b34c-b85cdbfbffa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352378} {:answer/id #uuid "bed79f4a-0cfa-5836-b842-a428a2d36729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353200} {:answer/id #uuid "e434e2a7-7b61-54c5-849e-eed49afb4c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353557} {:answer/id #uuid "ee197f33-2abf-50b9-aba9-dab21f8c8667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353640} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d88795-6e20-592b-9c42-a38587d0b059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3d88795-6e20-592b-9c42-a38587d0b059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353699} :answer-field/id #uuid "a938552d-1615-51f8-8da6-069af5c0fef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7877d68-b9fc-5193-a68e-4210074e245a" :question/math-academy-id "q-88495" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2$ and $x=6.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,6),\\,(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(0,2)$ and $(6,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca39c86-c237-57c2-8d8c-dda21442afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355347} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce90c57-4877-5774-9681-0f9aa958e585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355909} {:answer/id #uuid "9280f538-12fe-5a96-8f3c-4ff956dd093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355957} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb85bb0-6fa4-59b2-ad41-5c5cd393e251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356060} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bb5ddb-c925-56df-85f5-227622e09a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ca39c86-c237-57c2-8d8c-dda21442afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355347} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ad4582-1eee-5ebd-8785-6a3cf5b49efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db570458-831d-5df7-87a7-8b916c4f12ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-88918" :question/problem "A pet center registered $34$ dogs and $29$ cats on a particular day. Of the dogs registered that day, $12$ were vaccinated. Of the cats registered that day, $15$ were vaccinated. What is the probability that a randomly selected pet registered that day was vaccinated?" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet was vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(V\\cap D),$ we take the number of dogs who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(12)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(34+29=63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap D)=\\frac{12}{63}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(V\\cap {D}^{′}),$ we take the number of cats who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(15)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap {D}^{′})=\\frac{15}{63}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{63}+\\frac{15}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5950026d-5e37-5043-bda6-0342549648c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355456} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac5c4c3-dc8d-5605-8fa6-8565bc86be09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355892} {:answer/id #uuid "b282e5ed-d10f-59a4-b2e2-6099c86a3c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356236} {:answer/id #uuid "b5882c3b-b469-59b3-b2af-d3143215e0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356260} {:answer/id #uuid "da1a7e88-6458-5d61-879c-1e515aebfa3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5882c3b-b469-59b3-b2af-d3143215e0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356260} :answer-field/id #uuid "199f2e03-e3c7-5645-88aa-b35bd25544bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fdc0332-d2c9-5e37-b448-51acd09ee37d" :question/math-academy-id "q-88978" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $55%$ are produced by machine $B.$ If $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $55%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.08,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.55) \\\\  & =0.058.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177abed1-3340-52f4-b1b1-3e9d3c91a06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354875} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce948d9-38e5-5268-a841-288391c7dc4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356063} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2bdc3-4057-5d74-97aa-8279dd26fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356091} {:answer/id #uuid "b51115cb-95b0-5606-8be1-1cd205702798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356257} {:answer/id #uuid "c42cb318-2ebc-50d6-8303-ec19ea35ed9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2bdc3-4057-5d74-97aa-8279dd26fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356091} :answer-field/id #uuid "73d73bcd-8256-5970-b6ac-230469df04c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e058ddbe-425b-5426-85a0-6f69cb521a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-89027" :question/problem "John keeps his marbles in two boxes, box $A$ and box $B.$ Of the marbles in his collection, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of them are in box $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them are in box $B.$ If $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, what is the probability that a randomly selected marble in John's collection is red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the chosen marble is red, and let $A$ be the event that the marble was selected from box $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the marble was selected from box $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marble is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the marbles are in box $A$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ are in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(R|{A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{4})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2566a906-660a-5539-8fd2-506a252beb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.637" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354977} {:answer/id #uuid "357ee747-8ccf-5b3a-ad24-9fe9d1620072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355146} {:answer/id #uuid "494958d9-9794-5e66-b1da-63cf9ff33c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.623" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355311} {:answer/id #uuid "654869f5-50fd-5413-ada4-6ad56a42a98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355563} {:answer/id #uuid "e463b964-6231-5f45-aec0-6341c1449646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.535" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357ee747-8ccf-5b3a-ad24-9fe9d1620072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355146} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0e9367-128c-5d29-9e01-6dd7b04cb3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc55985a-a406-5bf4-8dc7-f8f925865628" :question/math-academy-id "q-89040" :question/problem "In a gas station, $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.62)(0.42)+(0.55)(0.58) \\\\  & ≈0.579\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08821013-ff7e-5926-9170-5488f8c55f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354741} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb68099-ca5b-5b42-b7ff-66e2acd2597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354925} {:answer/id #uuid "2643da86-d4fb-5e40-b26b-e15ed509e6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354986} {:answer/id #uuid "432f5f04-984d-536d-b96e-2ac2c03b60e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355263} {:answer/id #uuid "a8eaf4dd-5dd6-5060-9c67-da16eec7be7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb68099-ca5b-5b42-b7ff-66e2acd2597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354925} :answer-field/id #uuid "448782f1-c860-52d3-b0bf-0b4fa3b86ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa3e12df-92c7-5fd3-840c-629e20a2f42c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89043" :question/problem "A student sits a multiple-choice test. Each question on the test has $n$ possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer to $70%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that they do not know, and the probability that a randomly selected question from the test is answered correctly is $0.76,$ what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knows the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student doesn't know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $70%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.7,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has $n$ possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected question is answered correctly is $0.76,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.76.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\ 0.76 & =(1)(0.70)+(\\frac{1}{n})(0.3) \\\\ 0.76 & =0.70+\\frac{0.3}{n} \\\\ \\frac{0.3}{n} & =0.06 \\\\ n & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test has $5$ possible answers for each question."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7acda339-8e7a-5d97-8d07-87d56f55cf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355742} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac1d3b7-75a1-5c1c-8ff2-b5129406317f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356039} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd7d09b-b5f0-5551-884a-1cbb0b928521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356071} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b2c84-13f4-5082-b461-631d5e9b5fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356376} {:answer/id #uuid "e20a7c7c-2570-5ddb-b5bf-ae180319b6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c42b2c84-13f4-5082-b461-631d5e9b5fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356376} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d03e9b-4d9b-5f17-8196-2161f649cd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e146c7f5-1f60-52aa-a388-c884e771fa07" :question/math-academy-id "q-89044" :question/problem "Jacob has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ In account $A$ he receives $40%$ of his emails and he receives the remaining $60%$ in account $B.$ Approximately $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam and $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $60%$ in account $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.40,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S)=0.36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ec3618-ccd9-584a-a671-2d5604a02992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354840} {:answer/id #uuid "35323479-c980-5e35-8a43-4987fdb1d440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355140} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac8d006-37eb-5ce9-b169-71af759ce8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355741} {:answer/id #uuid "836c2510-2c98-5336-8916-b05ea6092d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355823} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5dcc0b-b087-57d3-a0dd-557c5d8b3697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b5dcc0b-b087-57d3-a0dd-557c5d8b3697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7424e924-2e89-51e9-90d0-aa5b63cbb997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a852c2d-1240-54fc-a444-5143c0301a19" :question/math-academy-id "q-89051" :question/problem "Daniel has a desk with two drawers, drawer $A$ and drawer $B.$ Of the total amount of pens that he has, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of them are in drawer $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B.$ Also, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, and Daniel knows that $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens in both drawers are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue?" :question/worked-solution "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen pen is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the pen was selected from drawer $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the pen was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the pens are in drawer $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{5},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U|A)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens on the desk drawers are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(U|{D}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+P(U|{A}^{′})(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{10-9}{15} \\\\ P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue is $\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5848156b-2126-5df0-a070-7ef7a8d662a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355448} {:answer/id #uuid "7645e9f3-7d9d-5629-ab00-2e0f116ecf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355701} {:answer/id #uuid "9abf19fb-8870-5329-9f48-ed4d9aab7a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356038} {:answer/id #uuid "d88e4a88-acd4-56a4-9f2c-d456e379db5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356562} {:answer/id #uuid "fce56101-6a94-5cd5-9556-af616a864969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7645e9f3-7d9d-5629-ab00-2e0f116ecf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355701} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b0e6df-f21f-5057-802c-e00f1f95ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a328572c-0653-5db2-9ac5-0c926d0fcf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-89571" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a311471-e674-54a4-86e3-f08282feadef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355013} {:answer/id #uuid "54abc358-2297-5ed9-b224-29301c40a4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355415} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6d01e4-bfa3-50db-a7b7-a29e6a52f8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355621} {:answer/id #uuid "7530aeac-58e5-5f70-95bf-fb6661c02f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355694} {:answer/id #uuid "cd50e26c-5b63-5639-97d8-28143eece290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a311471-e674-54a4-86e3-f08282feadef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355013} :answer-field/id #uuid "594b5785-90a7-57fd-a175-4e46a81151d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c30e5682-d2e0-5625-9166-382c2b2b89c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89651" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2}\\le 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator ${x}^{4}+2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${x}^{4}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+3x & \\le 2{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ 3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183ac72d-04bc-515b-a1ac-1dba09d05681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354883} {:answer/id #uuid "1a07ca52-d09f-5a5e-8553-6936dcdc170e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354895} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c10717-0f93-5b60-a330-54a52701e532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356166} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a6b29e-a6ed-5467-80a8-6c7e42eaa60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356553} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d854de-28f5-5eed-80d3-28fbabaecee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d854de-28f5-5eed-80d3-28fbabaecee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356814} :answer-field/id #uuid "725ee54e-e664-5302-ae60-3eaafa3f93a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a029723c-ae65-5f0f-afa0-5d78d651bfe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-89661" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6{x}^{5}+18x>3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all terms to the left-hand side and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}+18x & >3x \\\\ 6{x}^{5}+15x & >0 \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{4}+5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{x}^{4}+5$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{4}+5)}{2{x}^{4}+5} & >\\frac{0}{2{x}^{4}+5} \\\\ 3x & >0 \\\\ x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c5a493-ee80-5d5e-b4b0-bdf3b20459ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354681} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4dea9a-9bd3-51a5-8046-4c3d74307db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355509} {:answer/id #uuid "921a5bd9-efe5-504c-9ae8-b4a7be12dfc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355954} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0256e2-c4fd-5c65-b792-d5f1ea534612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356187} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd090e1-d3f5-53c4-a92b-77c21f38298c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921a5bd9-efe5-504c-9ae8-b4a7be12dfc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355954} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fc496e-50a9-5e01-af36-876a28d5a653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2bee22d-7ec5-5d27-a0a4-8fc204928c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-89676" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)<0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $-3{x}^{2}-4$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)}{-3{x}^{2}-4} & >\\frac{0}{-3{x}^{2}-4} \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c2a5d4-298c-5631-a9d7-aba5b729e118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404463} {:answer/id #uuid "01bc8bf9-9741-5be3-bf6c-963968a707d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y\\le 3 \\\\ y\\ge 2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404464} {:answer/id #uuid "da29e297-529e-5fe2-b38a-d2962daba4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404465} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5ca256-25d0-5c51-b2d0-61bafb216142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404466} {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404467} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5be21f-60e7-597b-83dc-3c20339aa469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "262609c0-5557-5285-b43c-f82fdb8825a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-90054" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8c1d5902a76a68e5f8358d4c3668e1be184ec7241184df4ff1363d41995f3aa.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $x+y=3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+3,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+y>3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict.  Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338a182a-eee2-5984-9c16-ac8f5df92c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb0ea125af03527a6e3111fc638f5fd9a4efaa39bc7b48a82e2efcfcfb0b696b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394829} {:answer/id #uuid "3faab0ae-2527-51e9-960b-f170f93df96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27ba3ad2783e64a6b29d5ea488d63fc634d2a43ee01d6a77c4802da8bc06b641.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394830} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ca9e1d-7076-5b73-a120-7f365e92f4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5428cead5e96d930e52a833942a1c4452c43f3e2f6ba7c6fc39758ee83e8d81.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394831} {:answer/id #uuid "55b6c46d-d1d1-5590-9b0c-83f0e901feca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/197ae7933ece17a22bdf17c2397053541891a789892813599930de273496cd78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394832} {:answer/id #uuid "547435a9-62b0-54fe-88c5-c2df65965648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547435a9-62b0-54fe-88c5-c2df65965648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394833} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c907422-6a43-51a3-a689-8ba380b4f66e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12b718de-1f28-5ec6-b73e-0c704e48e717" :question/math-academy-id "q-90174" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x \\\\ y\\le -2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To graph the first inequality $y\\le 2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=2x.$\n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nTo graph the second inequality $y\\le -2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=-2x.$ \n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nThe intersection of the solutions is the following:\n\n![](images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e3339b-5c2d-5e02-8e56-922ef2cb88bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404470} {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404471} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93daa1-24f9-58db-9545-6bf0487628c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y\\ge \\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404472} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf061e8-e3ee-5abf-a78a-299067a79064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y<\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404473} {:answer/id #uuid "6051d13a-7c7c-5804-88c5-53711a5cb6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404471} :answer-field/id #uuid "94914b35-e16f-5398-9a50-4baddb153e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66b0d8c5-2487-53cc-b840-b06edcc12d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-90323" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e863789ff989ee1cb3d3fe6c22fe0d4daa1dac76d6fbfbebdad801006ca4c969.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=4x+3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<4x+3.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daea2326-2376-5164-b217-f74ca9ead6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404477} {:answer/id #uuid "8b73faca-4331-5468-aa5b-5f3ece1295f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\le -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404478} {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404479} {:answer/id #uuid "b45d0b54-a0ec-5724-b49e-549e13b6d4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404480} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2a88ff-6f4a-5ac9-a039-72c31f12f559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404479} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86fe5f2-d23c-5598-bb24-07f9f597ec1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e5e2c49-2afd-5324-972b-43974f8bfdd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90327" :question/problem "![](images/76/762e53967ddb3ccddfa1c9b7a2aa303d66fc1b761c9be8f409c0b91ecb0201de.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities represents the shaded region shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0916d67e-384b-59fb-ac4a-2ddf93d64157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354749} {:answer/id #uuid "55aac323-4890-5652-9c14-ec5060cf4dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355426} {:answer/id #uuid "cd50177d-cb69-545f-8f2e-01e3fb7d4f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356461} {:answer/id #uuid "efb97d98-6dc2-5717-8476-8989347340ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356760} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0e7b6b-cdec-5b74-af38-736cb3453cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb97d98-6dc2-5717-8476-8989347340ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356760} :answer-field/id #uuid "307b2650-ca15-5ba8-85f2-8f1872a2d02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "477fb1bb-4cfe-5309-9a32-c387bdafb083" :question/math-academy-id "q-90485" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are  $x=-1,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435945f7-a56e-5841-afc3-c726b82c1505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355265} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3a667a-38b7-50e0-a46e-6fff2329af48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355352} {:answer/id #uuid "a38c0513-a659-5214-aac2-7c8d2a08f035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356114} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a5568e-6360-5a68-b432-e00967ac1041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356424} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9233b9-fb65-5647-b8a9-5da2e6c4e508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a38c0513-a659-5214-aac2-7c8d2a08f035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356114} :answer-field/id #uuid "47a3f0de-2303-502b-97c2-40412f6529a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b9462f0-7c1a-53bf-b48b-ceebd886cef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-90536" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e6159dd-c12e-5882-be17-815b4cc9ae52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "720ce82d-536f-52e4-a924-9dc9994eace0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355669} {:answer/id #uuid "9c357947-0335-531e-b792-b57594efd6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356049} {:answer/id #uuid "a14eace9-3890-5be9-b73b-56227bff4efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356096} {:answer/id #uuid "a60dca9c-200d-526d-95e8-ae5ecfbe0621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356135} {:answer/id #uuid "d317e9da-0cf9-523f-af7e-fdda914c3077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a14eace9-3890-5be9-b73b-56227bff4efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356096} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0524cb-a296-51c4-96c8-ba5fc5980ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1da47db3-4817-571a-b409-df8d62fa2bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-92875" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{5x+12} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{5x+12})^{3} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ 5x+12 & =27 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110202e8-093f-58a8-9e0d-f6d0462fe4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354817} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec7982b-e6e2-5127-9750-dd495e4fc60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355367} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5cd5d7-65b0-52b7-a3d8-f1ee2974f09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355615} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8dd3e6-8366-51e5-8487-6e2a2ed44084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355616} {:answer/id #uuid "bd971419-1d83-5381-8060-ebd02a942d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd971419-1d83-5381-8060-ebd02a942d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356325} :answer-field/id #uuid "388f326d-f720-5ae1-b8d8-e7be69c7f1e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab1d8b5d-ee01-5c7e-86ea-f76938391b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-92930" :question/problem "What is the $y$-intercept of $y=-5{e}^{-{x}^{3}}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5{e}^{-(0)^{3}}+3 \\\\  & =-5{e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =-5(1)+3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3346b133-465c-5f7c-8f6e-ed203cfa9c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355115} {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} {:answer/id #uuid "7016e321-e75a-5e1d-9c03-93b9317abb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355653} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef13b5a-fed1-5020-9287-b841ede6880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355925} {:answer/id #uuid "e3296fe7-7c8e-558c-bc20-fd9a8725aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd144ebe-3889-52dd-aeed-40781f7c5ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e35f675f-b059-5f5e-9a19-8ac6f6c3e6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-92963" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x-3}+2.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/86/8646efab1e707e57bd8ff93b5f3f7b1336ae23588abf2d423a45bbcf702f86ab.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ba6113-d9e6-554c-a5ab-303df960703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355256} {:answer/id #uuid "59222bb1-9b57-550e-b74f-18960c6bcc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355454} {:answer/id #uuid "b9602130-4235-5679-81e3-ffa15cdfc320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356288} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c96c1e-b278-534e-bfd6-d6d031552911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356557} {:answer/id #uuid "e710d78c-3d06-5abd-ba31-927a221020a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ba6113-d9e6-554c-a5ab-303df960703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355256} :answer-field/id #uuid "195e4818-64d5-542b-b720-a7f4bc8b73d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43f0b9fb-d57d-5094-ba73-d0cc6a85c5c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-92988" :question/problem "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=2({e}^{2x})-5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc2af8bae74f163f0dd4eae74fd90fc6c5460cae552c7e5072c83521444aea29.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-5$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15eaa20a-798d-591d-9791-7bfbe7399837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354865} {:answer/id #uuid "3d31328a-39b6-52ea-807f-d59024fad9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355210} {:answer/id #uuid "4560de0c-70f4-51f5-a9a3-63faf56a8b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355280} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d7c148-b989-57f1-b1ac-86ad209009b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356254} {:answer/id #uuid "e7836302-9e53-590b-81c4-7e3b4b7e7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4560de0c-70f4-51f5-a9a3-63faf56a8b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1ba9a3-81a9-5dc9-9598-2c7b40e1e779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1738a08a-0e6c-5ca9-b9ee-772359397eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-92995" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-{e}^{4}=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${e}^{4}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-\\cancel{{e}^{4}}+\\cancel{{e}^{4}} & =0+{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $e$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024cb00d-23a5-5998-a7e2-0ef6f494abe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354676} {:answer/id #uuid "35ac62bd-1fa9-5ff6-bc3a-d5415133874f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355149} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff56156-dec0-5625-887c-a6a00810f413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355376} {:answer/id #uuid "5045dc71-23e2-5c2f-aeb5-cf26fed94158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355380} {:answer/id #uuid "d787633f-7310-59a3-a889-9935ea02f4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d787633f-7310-59a3-a889-9935ea02f4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356550} :answer-field/id #uuid "742646f8-b0b0-5de6-aa67-6e561c90e6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc356c61-c382-5786-b505-51f9c59fb0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-92998" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=4{e}^{2x-1}-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x-1}-8=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1}-8 & =0 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1}-\\cancel{8}+\\cancel{8} & =0+8 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1} & =8 \\\\ {e}^{2x-1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d59733f-3396-5d59-98a9-3f2e0ccfbbc4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f7a022-23ac-53c8-a81d-072f503d8fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355270} {:answer/id #uuid "4d646465-2c5a-5ca4-b933-806b037217a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355353} {:answer/id #uuid "519ae3d6-ebd2-56c4-b611-9fffaf2c2e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355392} {:answer/id #uuid "67a53918-48e6-591c-9e5b-8d73080dc4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355583} {:answer/id #uuid "d5647ae4-bc8a-51dc-8452-b54a04fb4241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5647ae4-bc8a-51dc-8452-b54a04fb4241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356532} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1640760-c633-5dbb-a8f2-26644e16ddb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45faf24d-552b-523b-9d7b-94bb05598c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-9302" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f31b9f180b1b766451685428fc289766832fe5d0c7d8902a601ebf060eab0e7.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 0=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-10|\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\  & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df12665-ee8e-589e-bd84-ddf20099fe58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404546} {:answer/id #uuid "d09c688e-d77f-5dc2-a795-7353b6d5cf30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404547} {:answer/id #uuid "996332e4-ee9d-5bd3-bfe0-10173565755e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404548} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7008c7-4ccb-5f75-80d4-933508b111d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404549} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb2712-9786-5f37-ba7a-d303316974c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996332e4-ee9d-5bd3-bfe0-10173565755e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404548} :answer-field/id #uuid "207a57e3-40d0-5996-94ed-9587a6c43801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb7d641-51be-5b64-b25b-f4aefccbd21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93099" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8 & =0 \\\\ -4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11} & =8 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{5x-11} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b40c4dd3-1d79-54b0-823b-dbcec9da032f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394869} {:answer/id #uuid "56beaf5c-1cdd-555a-929d-05414265748d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394870} {:answer/id #uuid "cec913c6-692b-55f2-a18c-269be0d2cbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394871} {:answer/id #uuid "242e6691-583b-5dcd-9ea4-1ea760a5e7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394872} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3041e7-9b3f-53a2-9125-16a47ceb3ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "242e6691-583b-5dcd-9ea4-1ea760a5e7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394872} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5dd29b7-7c45-5120-8f4f-b750b0c0e6da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d119284d-a155-58d7-994e-d134bc9c1879" :question/math-academy-id "q-93343" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d0b6b63883b58ecce92e395380b140b32946a4bb037e1829544539749412af9.png)\n\nLiz deconstructs a lantern and gets the net shown above. In the figure, the areas of two of its faces are shown. What is the total surface area of the lantern?" :question/worked-solution "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ triangle (base) and $3$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+3\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 5+3\\times 13 \\\\  & =5+39 \\\\  & =44\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268ee764-f4ba-5bb4-b9a6-25d0217bbe25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354988} {:answer/id #uuid "46057ddc-18e7-5373-90a9-8e327b81e327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355286} {:answer/id #uuid "9818d993-5599-51c2-be03-5470aa4ffc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356007} {:answer/id #uuid "99b6d8a4-b90d-5fc6-aad9-00a4aaa9f246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356029} {:answer/id #uuid "e518197b-b17c-5f90-b633-521d029c1507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46057ddc-18e7-5373-90a9-8e327b81e327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355286} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bf6b9f-ca8a-544b-9d4e-a0a158f07d3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e38779e8-5306-5b5e-a170-45db52e7f21d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94457" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10.8.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=10.5,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{10.8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=10.5$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{10.5-6}{\\sqrt{10.8}} \\\\  & ≈1.37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202f2ede-33d8-5d23-a897-b75e4b1cc459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354933} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb96c7-be37-57f3-af85-a3d9641697ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355348} {:answer/id #uuid "4e12e047-e3d4-5a77-974e-be93c594e33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355357} {:answer/id #uuid "b276c159-4f0a-5ba8-bcf1-27b7f26ed839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356235} {:answer/id #uuid "baadf541-5610-5499-940a-702e8f2b4f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202f2ede-33d8-5d23-a897-b75e4b1cc459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354933} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb393c6-1b84-5f14-9647-ac0b4335c2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "694368b5-e718-5efc-b12c-37e7e99e29bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94460" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=28$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=3.60.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=14,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{3.60}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=14$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14-28}{\\sqrt{3.60}} \\\\  & ≈-7.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bcca9d-4ee4-558c-ac6d-3dba6fed8a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356239} {:answer/id #uuid "d8efc532-df6e-5410-bc77-a7e5779ef469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356568} {:answer/id #uuid "db1c6fb0-f620-582c-930f-ddf023b658c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356592} {:answer/id #uuid "e8af3748-e949-5d0a-b0f5-6cde72841fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356698} {:answer/id #uuid "f9242971-1fcf-5df3-b81b-b36d9bd54094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8efc532-df6e-5410-bc77-a7e5779ef469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f71beb9-8e13-54e1-a94f-41a8e740e60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "530121a9-23c4-5834-af6f-63e88a6adfa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94471" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=20$ and $z$-score $z=2.2.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=20,$ $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5,$ and $z=2.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 20 & =14.5+2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.5 & =2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1faf19fd-0dd5-586e-926d-6340b195ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354930} {:answer/id #uuid "49216ee7-3d5e-58e3-a5fb-5cdd2a0d69f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355310} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6197b1-b81b-52af-b216-72cc6a553860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355897} {:answer/id #uuid "d597713f-c6b1-5820-b22a-210e4633ed35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356535} {:answer/id #uuid "e81a51df-4cdd-5475-bfa1-e5313586a540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1faf19fd-0dd5-586e-926d-6340b195ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354930} :answer-field/id #uuid "366f682e-3735-536c-b4a3-11af61adcace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fa2412f-8859-5e81-9fb7-100702a88998" :question/math-academy-id "q-94519" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of ballet dancers is $45\\text{ kg}.$ One of the dancers in the ballet group weighs $38\\text{ kg}$, and her $z$-score is $-2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the weight of the dancers in the ballet group." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=38,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=45,$ and $z=-2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 38 & =45+(-2.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -7 & =-2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-7}{-2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the ballet dancer's weights is $2.8\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7c8253-2398-5844-ab87-9f569178c088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358387} {:answer/id #uuid "604bb718-8188-5706-9d5e-bc5e825254c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358594} {:answer/id #uuid "64729ba5-db61-5904-a7f3-3348bf688c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358631} {:answer/id #uuid "95c21078-c3b5-5a29-a41b-fa3dd5c92b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409277}]} {:answer/id #uuid "9903b498-b87b-528c-9348-0e7f32f90a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c21078-c3b5-5a29-a41b-fa3dd5c92b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4becacdb-3199-5e03-8693-24305826113a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21e5af7d-c901-55dc-94b7-2ce8f4a4e5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96663" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19447168-1474-5be9-aac6-ad97fed12bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360937} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} {:answer/id #uuid "26497c17-1e52-5f13-9672-7d7541e882bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361001} {:answer/id #uuid "2e772d5a-11ce-5758-badf-fef6f326e8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361044} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84d4c8-52d6-5fed-ade3-9113944fe3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a007c32a-4b84-5c4e-9daf-279e1005a6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a38c82cd-ff4b-53d8-85bc-f102ff90d451" :question/math-academy-id "q-9785" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Str