8f73-5b80-9ca3-ee3a40eda45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97949" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{3}{4x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{3}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{3}{4x}.$\n\n![](images/76/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac99bf6-327a-5511-b7a4-734f9c76a5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b9683bcc1b559505141d45b9007ef45011adee98b33730ffad1876a462d9abc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395372} {:answer/id #uuid "d352e845-d658-517b-b4e5-85b07a890741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/804eac3df07358686b859f20630acc5344f82ff0c71dc80e6320ab2f7b2313f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395373} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d701f-35d2-53fb-9bb7-6e53d677c6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a2af9de8793d7f2e527721b3874ac6d49d25d0a1e496d3cd62ecf2710b4ab932.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395374} {:answer/id #uuid "91f3d40c-2faf-5526-b31d-531299ba0057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395375} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ab1f08-3af2-5eaf-b009-717e15cf1eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40490df9d6ec4cc44759b0265e9355dfe6edba66028e61d6e5c36c0d839388d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f3d40c-2faf-5526-b31d-531299ba0057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dcbea8f-b000-5d95-8095-fceedf72d8ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5276417-1d46-5a8a-a5e9-31a83ac43586" :question/math-academy-id "q-98004" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the left to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$\n\n![](images/04/049f9c306c007026bc72e5f00d925cf22d80d94049aa82e3c81d7fc7c289249f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.$\n\n![](images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b1c11b-b4b1-536c-8889-3a3eb09882dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395425} {:answer/id #uuid "c96ad5b8-0210-5052-9081-f410de18a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395426} {:answer/id #uuid "398a8fd3-2eed-529b-9541-d6be85bc82d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395427} {:answer/id #uuid "04663b1a-f275-5578-853a-d2e34a0425ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395428} {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea09eb90-beeb-5d00-94ad-88249fe8aeca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ffaa1-a202-507f-aa7e-4d95a973e5e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98142" :question/problem "Calculate the distance between the points $(3w,2w,7w)$ and $(-w,6w,3w),$ given that $w$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have ${{(3w,2w,7w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-w,6w,3w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Applying the distance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-w-3w{)}^{2}+(6w-2w{)}^{2}+(3w-7w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4w{)}^{2}+(4w{)}^{2}+(-4w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $w$ is positive, we have $|w|=w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nd=4\\sqrt{3}\\,w.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2dcd36-d37e-5f97-a4eb-f4a0e43b3d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361244} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8ef0d3-446a-5d6e-ada9-16f2db45756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361378} {:answer/id #uuid "b8feab6f-aab4-50c9-a460-d7a38f586edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361661} {:answer/id #uuid "db29ac2c-5b62-5efc-a993-5086c236ea43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361812} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcd738f-f001-53ce-9a93-cd0317226734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a2dcd36-d37e-5f97-a4eb-f4a0e43b3d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361244} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d34327-185f-56e1-8f43-80ae24e1544f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbbbaf02-7f2f-5863-a78f-3470c810112d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98161" :question/problem "Find the principal $7$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{7}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07de7854-21ae-5eb4-a781-39eaf6920d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360866} {:answer/id #uuid "11e0c7a1-7e38-5347-b91a-0182668e0eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360905} {:answer/id #uuid "2cca5a8b-b099-5b27-bb51-5a8a76591d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361037} {:answer/id #uuid "67064f5b-20ba-52d5-8416-dc7de2bf88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57.55%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361300} {:answer/id #uuid "6755f482-de75-5ebc-aed7-289483aad1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6755f482-de75-5ebc-aed7-289483aad1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361302} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9455a6-4ffc-529e-9d61-00633942c052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2633ed-ce6d-535e-9977-3b543de5d2f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-9846" :question/problem "In a trigonometry test, the class instructor decided that students whose $z$-score is greater than or equal to $-1$ will pass the test. The mean percent score $x$ of the students of the class is $\\overset{¯}{x}=64.3%$ with a standard deviation of $\\sigma =5.25%.$ Find the minimum passing score." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=64.3,$ $z=-1,$ and $\\sigma =5.25$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =64.3+(-1)\\cdot 5.25 \\\\  & =64.3-5.25 \\\\  & =59.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the minimum passing score is $59.05%.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118da805-8d4d-5712-a36c-cd77c1c5bcc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360902} {:answer/id #uuid "221ed9dd-6363-58f5-a13f-28ea35802178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360987} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} {:answer/id #uuid "3432d0b7-a400-5e44-af3e-694272f1758a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361067} {:answer/id #uuid "7608caaa-0a67-5ff3-8a2a-4143e6b8492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca620ff8-9a8a-5856-bc6f-86f00cb0ed6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2197596-2744-5571-ba37-b2f03d9b6d9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98716" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,\\,x+2,\\,x(x+2))=x(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ x(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}) & =x(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2x(x+2)}{x}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{4x(x+2)}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x}}-\\frac{x\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{x(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x(x+2)}} \\\\ 2(x+2)-x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)-x & =4 \\\\ 2x+4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae8b541b-6c2c-5c34-8f52-8d8bc5c341df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404969} {:answer/id #uuid "83b7d222-6b21-5f46-baad-0be2919bb086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404970} {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404971} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d814eb-c7d7-579f-9607-8d549d7bda4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404972} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3e5eca-a564-5d66-b064-7f655185e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404971} :answer-field/id #uuid "20839a78-520c-5f1d-8f7a-a6481c46623d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a08e4af-7f63-5439-b490-91f36e22b19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99032" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+24x+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+24x+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+7) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd281f17-99d6-5be7-a5a7-e5d777ed4a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404976} {:answer/id #uuid "80c4a5f6-a26f-5884-b87b-97b1c3d00708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404977} {:answer/id #uuid "1972cef3-5e06-5006-a5e9-98532f424f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404978} {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404979} {:answer/id #uuid "1df8665d-93b7-5a6b-9a93-0bb764b11fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404979} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a891f36-9832-5e16-a500-7251af8a1737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb13e83e-9cd0-53cb-aaf0-21f542a77f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99050" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e62997e2-1f0c-5d4b-9eb1-4b41bb6ab374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404983} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404984} {:answer/id #uuid "01c6ef37-e7d0-563b-82d0-0dd75adf8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404985} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca2e0a4-dadf-59ca-94f1-7367e00a0944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404986} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a5af9-9a13-510f-84ca-0b15cfce2ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404984} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da6d1b2-a92b-5381-b98b-6a6c794e9358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42dd53a1-4ccb-5086-adb5-70b77ded4e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99053" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065b8702-9c1f-5408-a110-0b6b1f931f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404997} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404998} {:answer/id #uuid "625ac297-0116-50dc-b32e-593a5c25fd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404999} {:answer/id #uuid "2f10324a-c1cb-5f86-99bd-f736b0ed8050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405000} {:answer/id #uuid "acea3269-c2cc-5d39-87be-8ac3b32540bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404998} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf1d3ea-9362-58c4-b795-b8796b5019b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99056" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4dc8da-0af6-521a-94e2-29efe502a4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e2a1274fc65341a659bb76923bb69677cc33bad1b78dfdf64ba97e0c8a00fb9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068423} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cfcb00-3352-501b-8c96-ce26e77b5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f3a1cac41b2f9d6773226d76722508e6d7f368a42a0f7e86a5d8407d6f42c149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068424} {:answer/id #uuid "23e003a0-4faa-5012-ab5b-6a16e4b56306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79b496d28a0136f46e594a1b8dfddffcf4ed72557ded65825